<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques-François de Brouillan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a>–<a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jacques-François de Monbeton de Brouillan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques-François de Brouillan was a French officer and colonial governor, governor of Plaisance (Placentia) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> from 1691 to 1701 and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>. At Plaisance he oversaw Fort Saint-Louis and the cod fishery, defended the colony against English attacks and took part in the campaign of 1696–1697 against English settlements alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a>.</p></div>
